e expresses a **small amount with positive emphasis**, not “as a very large quantity”. It gives neither an exact count nor a claim that the amount is zero.","B":"The phrase expresses a **small amount with positive emphasis**, not “as a stated exact number”. It gives neither an exact count nor a claim that the amount is zero.","C":"a little means some, often with positive emphasis. It does not specify an exact number or amount.","D":"The phrase expresses a **small amount with positive emphasis**, not “as exactly zero”. It gives neither an exact count nor a claim that the amount is zero."}}},{"id":"6bc13f0d-d1ee-5f4d-a02a-087e0ca2de27","topicSlug":"small-quantities","level":"A2","prompt":"For measuring liquid fuel, which phrase correctly uses a small-quantity expression with “fuel”?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"a few fuel"},{"id":"B","text":"many fuel"},{"id":"C","text":"several fuel"},{"id":"D","text":"a bit of fuel"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"A few goes with plural countable nouns. A little and a bit of go with uncountable nouns. These expressions can present the amount positively. Focus on **noun fit** in this question.","explanation":"A bit of goes with uncountable nouns: a bit of fuel.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"noun-fit","contextKey":"quantity-49","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-small-quantities"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"e9f28b34aeeb7e8f9fe57ebe7dd3a020b79b5e8534e6d0faf384ef231492aabb","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **noun fit**. A few goes with plural countable nouns. A little and a bit of go with uncountable nouns. These expressions can present the amount positively.","A bit of goes with uncountable nouns: a bit of fuel.","Choose **a bit of fuel**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**a few fuel** fits plural countable nouns. This question uses the other kind of noun in its stated sense. A bit of goes with uncountable nouns: a bit of fuel.","B":"**many fuel** fits plural countable nouns. This question uses the other kind of noun in its stated sense. A bit of goes with uncountable nouns: a bit of fuel.","C":"**several fuel** fits plural countable nouns. This question uses the other kind of noun in its stated sense. A bit of goes with uncountable nouns: a bit of fuel.","D":"A bit of goes with uncountable nouns: a bit of fuel."}}},{"id":"8233b076-097d-54a1-a171-34fd436eb17d","topicSlug":"small-quantities","level":"A2","prompt":"For measuring liquid soap, present a small available quantity positively: “We have ____ soap available.” Choose the small-quantity phrase.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"a few"},{"id":"B","text":"many"},{"id":"C","text":"a little"},{"id":"D","text":"few"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"A few goes with plural countable nouns. A little and a bit of go with uncountable nouns. These expressions can present the amount positively. Focus on **positive amount** in this question.","explanation":"a little presents a small quantity of soap positively. Use a little with uncountable nouns.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"positive-amount","contextKey":"quantity-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-small-quantities"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"17241bbf80e14e7d1a9e24464e5aaeb9ce8194b5bf71c8d4c002a4bffa24be89","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **positive amount**. A few goes with plural countable nouns. A little and a bit of go with uncountable nouns. These expressions can present the amount positively.","a little presents a small quantity of soap positively. Use a little with uncountable nouns.","Choose **a little**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**a few** fits plural countable nouns. This question uses the other kind of noun in its stated sense. a little presents a small quantity of soap positively. Use a little with uncountable nouns.","B":"**many** fits plural countable nouns. This question uses the other kind of noun in its stated sense. a little presents a small quantity of soap positively. Use a little with uncountable nouns.","C":"a little presents a small quantity of soap positively. Use a little with uncountable nouns.","D":"**few** fits plural countable nouns. This question uses the other kind of noun in its stated sense. a little presents a small quantity of soap positively. Use a little with uncountable nouns."}}},{"id":"ed1c0736-02f0-5dc0-adc2-4d6d2774ed9a","topicSlug":"small-quantities","level":"A2","prompt":"For measuring liquid soap, emphasise that the quantity is almost zero: “We have ____ soap, unfortunately.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"few"},{"id":"B","text":"very little"},{"id":"C","text":"a little"},{"id":"D","text":"a few"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Few with plural nouns and little or very little with uncountable nouns emphasise scarcity. The surrounding context tells us whether the quantity feels sufficient. Focus on **shortage** in this question.","explanation":"very little emphasises scarcity with uncountable soap.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"shortage","contextKey":"quantity-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-small-quantities"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"bf2b0ac09cdde08c5f9d0c4ddb9a141c6b83c4b11a7e68099cbdf635b0ea7dcf","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **shortage**. Few with plural nouns and little or very little with uncountable nouns emphasise scarcity. The surrounding context tells us whether the quantity feels sufficient.","very little emphasises scarcity with uncountable soap.","Choose **very little**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**few** fits plural countable nouns. This question uses the other kind of noun in its stated sense. very little emphasises scarcity with uncountable soap.","B":"very little emphasises scarcity with uncountable soap.","C":"The quantity-word family fits, but **a little** changes the emphasis: the prompt asks for scarcity, almost none. very little emphasises scarcity with uncountable soap.","D":"**a few** fits plural countable nouns. This question uses the other kind of noun in its stated sense. very little emphasises scarcity with uncountable soap."}}},{"id":"093eb6cc-b5fc-55f9-a9e0-e7714c9009d5","topicSlug":"small-quantities","level":"A2","prompt":"In a discussion about soap in the washing container, “We have a little soap” presents the amount in which way?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"As a stated exact number"},{"id":"B","text":"As a small quantity with positive emphasis"},{"id":"C","text":"As exactly zero"},{"id":"D","text":"As a very large quantity"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"A few and few have different emphasis despite both referring to small numbers. Do not treat all small quantities as shortages. Focus on **meaning** in this question.","explanation":"a little means some, often with positive emphasis. It does not specify an exact number or amount.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"meaning","contextKey":"quantity-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-small-quantities"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"5ebe1ecff7299d15b37089824e70e4da9a8ee9cb140951c85bfe19a419b3565f","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **meaning**. A few and few have different emphasis despite both referring to small numbers. Do not treat all small quantities as shortages.","a little means some, often with positive emphasis. It does not specify an exact number or amount.","Choose **As a small quantity with positive emphasis**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The phrase expresses a **small amount with positive emphasis**, not “as a stated exact number”. It gives neither an exact count nor a claim that the amount is zero.","B":"a little means some, often with positive emphasis. It does not specify an exact number or amount.","C":"The phrase expresses a **small amount with positive emphasis**, not “as exactly zero”. It gives neither an exact count nor a claim that the amount is zero.","D":"The phrase expresses a **small amount with positive emphasis**, not “as a very large quantity”. It gives neither an exact count nor a claim that the amount is zero."}}},{"id":"4a828528-fd0c-5476-a1a8-eb828f7f8569","topicSlug":"small-quantities","level":"A2","prompt":"For measuring liquid soap, which phrase correctly uses a small-quantity expression with “soap”?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"many soap"},{"id":"B","text":"several soap"},{"id":"C","text":"a bit of soap"},{"id":"D","text":"a few soap"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"A few goes with plural countable nouns. A little and a bit of go with uncountable nouns. These expressions can present the amount positively. Focus on **noun fit** in this question.","explanation":"A bit of goes with uncountable nouns: a bit of soap.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"noun-fit","contextKey":"quantity-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-small-quantities"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"a7b48951023fc26d0973e142b37b7da15f929afe88796c151ced2d1cff5d8eac","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **noun fit**. A few goes with plural countable nouns. A little and a bit of go with uncountable nouns. These expressions can present the amount positively.","A bit of goes with uncountable nouns: a bit of soap.","Choose **a bit of soap**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**many soap** fits plural countable nouns. This question uses the other kind of noun in its stated sense. A bit of goes with uncountable nouns: a bit of soap.","B":"**several soap** fits plural countable nouns. This question uses the other kind of noun in its stated sense. A bit of goes with uncountable nouns: a bit of soap.","C":"A bit of goes with uncountable nouns: a bit of soap.","D":"**a few soap** fits plural countable nouns. This question uses the other kind of noun in its stated sense. A bit of goes with uncountable nouns: a bit of soap."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Small-quantity words combine two decisions: whether the noun is countable and whether the speaker presents the amount positively or as a shortage. The tiny article a can change the emphasis substantially. These words do not normally give an exact number."],"rules":[{"title":"A positive small amount","explanation":"A few goes with plural countable nouns. A little and a bit of go with uncountable nouns. These expressions can present the amount positively.","examples":["A few volunteers can help.","We have a little energy left.","We have **a few** seats, so you can join us."],"pattern":"**a few** books / **few** books","paragraphs":["A few goes with plural count nouns and presents some as available. Few without a often stresses that the number is disappointingly small. The same facts can receive a different emphasis: a few friends sounds more positive than few friends."]},{"title":"Emphasising a shortage","explanation":"Few with plural nouns and little or very little with uncountable nouns emphasise scarcity. The surrounding context tells us whether the quantity feels sufficient.","examples":["Few volunteers signed up; we need more.","There is very little energy left.","We have **few** seats left, so book soon."],"pattern":"**a little** time / **very little** time","paragraphs":["A little goes with uncountable nouns and presents a small amount positively. Little or very little stresses scarcity. Do not use a few with ordinary uncountable advice or equipment. A unit phrase can make a countable expression: a few pieces of advice."]},{"title":"Read the meaning as well as the noun","explanation":"A few and few have different emphasis despite both referring to small numbers. Do not treat all small quantities as shortages.","examples":["We have a few useful ideas.","We have few useful ideas, unfortunately.","There is **a little** time, but **very little** space."],"pattern":"**a bit of** help","paragraphs":["A bit of is an informal small-amount phrase before an uncountable noun. A little can also modify an adjective or adverb in other structures. Identify the following word’s role before applying a noun-quantity rule."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"Only a few or only a little can make a small amount sound limiting even though a remains. Few/little are not exact synonyms of none: they typically mean a small quantity, possibly close to zero. In informal speech, not many or not much may be more natural than few or little in some contexts.","examples":["We have **a few** seats, so you can join us.","We have **few** seats left, so book soon.","There is **a little** time, but **very little** space."]}],"recap":["**a few** books / **few** books","**a little** time / **very little** time","**a bit of** help"]},"stage":"2 · Everyday descriptions","prerequisites":["countable-uncountable"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
